1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,167
Every security feature in this phase
eventually lands in the same place: a
filter chain that runs before your harbor
controllers. If you can narrate that
chain, configuration stops feeling like
folklore.

2
00:00:12,287 --> 00:00:22,103
Servlet containers have a filter chain.
Spring Security installs
DelegatingFilterProxy (named
springSecurityFilterChain) into that
chain.

3
00:00:22,223 --> 00:00:33,695
The proxy forwards to FilterChainProxy,
which selects a SecurityFilterChain bean
for the request — useful when you have
separate chains for /api/** and browser
ops consoles.

4
00:00:33,815 --> 00:00:46,895
Inside the chosen chain, a list of
security filters runs in a deliberate
order: channel security, CORS, CSRF,
authentication mechanisms, session
management, authorization, exception
translation, and more.

5
00:00:47,015 --> 00:01:02,807
Order is not academic. CORS must handle
preflight early. CSRF must see the request
before controllers mutate state.
Authentication filters must populate the
SecurityContext before authorization reads
it.

6
00:01:02,927 --> 00:01:10,463
ExceptionTranslationFilter converts
security exceptions into 401/403 responses
or login redirects.

7
00:01:10,583 --> 00:01:21,335
Insert a custom filter in the wrong
relative position and you will debug
symptoms that look like “Spring is broken”
when the chain is merely misordered. Look
at this java for a moment.

8
00:01:21,455 --> 00:01:30,215
You can list the filters at runtime by
enabling debug security or inspecting the
chain bean — useful when a custom
OncePerRequestFilter never fires.

9
00:01:30,335 --> 00:01:43,223
Relative placement APIs on HttpSecurity
(addFilterBefore, addFilterAfter,
addFilterAt) only make sense when you know
the anchor filter’s job. SecurityContext
persistence deserves a beat.

10
00:01:43,343 --> 00:01:51,911
In classic servlet apps, persistence
filters load and store context around the
request, often tied to the HTTP session
for form login.

11
00:01:52,031 --> 00:02:02,087
Stateless JWT APIs typically use a
session-creation policy of stateless:
authenticate from the bearer token every
request, do not create an HTTP session.

12
00:02:02,207 --> 00:02:08,735
Mixing those modes accidentally is a
common source of sticky sessions and
surprising logouts on the harbor edge.

13
00:02:08,855 --> 00:02:19,487
A misconception is treating
SecurityFilterChain as “the servlet
container’s only filters.” Your logging
and correlation filters may still sit
outside Spring Security.

14
00:02:19,608 --> 00:02:27,743
Another is adding ten custom filters that
each re-parse the Authorization header —
compose with providers and one
authentication filter instead.

15
00:02:27,864 --> 00:02:39,072
Phase 7 ends with a coherent servlet
security story: credentials become
Authentication, filters enforce
CSRF/CORS/authorization, method security
deepens domain rules.

16
00:02:39,192 --> 00:02:51,768
The stack so far assumes a thread-per-
request servlet model. The next phase asks
what changes when AIS vessel positions fan
in faster than threads can wait — and why
that model needs publishers instead of
eager values.

17
00:02:51,888 --> 00:02:54,743
That door is reactive programming.
